  "worked_solution": "To solve this inequality using the graphical method, we first need to move all of the terms to the left-hand side so that the right-hand side is $0.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{x}^{2} & >10x+12 \\\\ -2{x}^{2}-10x-12 & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we want to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola $y=-2{x}^{2}-10x-12$ lies **above** the $x$-axis. Let's find the roots of the parabola by factoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{x}^{2}-10x-12 & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+10x+12 & =0 \\\\ (2{x}^{2}+6x)+(4x+12) & =0 \\\\ 2x(x+3)+4(x+3) & =0 \\\\ (2x+4)(x+3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that the roots are $x=-3$ and $x=-2,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004892/assets/539e0e68f4a03c0bd4a309ebf2c4a00638d99c6eee233ca8f2cef3814e419c95.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving \n\n$$\n-2{x}^{2}-10x-12>0,\n$$\n\n \nwe look for the part of the parabola that is **above** the $x$-axis.\n\nBased on the graph, the solution is $-3<x<-2.$\n\nUsing interval notation, we write $x\\in (-3,-2).$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
